An optimal and elegant approach to solve this problem:

### Method Explanation

We have two operations:
1. `"00"` $\to$ `"10"` (reduces the count of `'0'`s by 1 and introduces a `'1'`)
2. `"10"` $\to$ `"01"` (moves a `'0'` to the left past a `'1'`)

Notice that operation 2 allows any `'0'` appearing after the first `'0'` to move leftwards until all `'0'`s form a contiguous block. 

Once all $k$ zeros are grouped together starting at the index of the first zero (`firstZero`), we can repeatedly apply operation 1 (`"00"` $\to$ `"10"`). Each application converts two adjacent `'0'`s into `"10"`. Doing this across all $k$ zeros results in $k - 1$ ones followed by a single zero.

Since we cannot eliminate the final zero (operation 1 always leaves one zero, and operation 2 preserves the count of zeros), the maximum string achievable will have:
- Exactly one `'0'`, placed as far right as possible.
- The maximum number of leading `'1'`s possible is `firstZero + (k - 1)`.
- Therefore, the single `'0'` will be located at index `firstZero + k - 1`, and all other positions will be `'1'`.

If the string contains no zeros, it cannot be changed and is already maximal.

### C# Source Code

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public string MaximumBinaryString(string binary) {
        int firstZero = binary.IndexOf('0');
        if (firstZero == -1) {
            return binary;
        }

        int zeroCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < binary.Length; i++) {
            if (binary[i] == '0') {
                zeroCount++;
            }
        }

        char[] result = new char[binary.Length];
        Array.Fill(result, '1');
        result[firstZero + zeroCount - 1] = '0';

        return new string(result);
    }
}
